# Food, carbs and meals

Nocturne keeps four separate things where a lot of diabetes software keeps one.
Understanding which is which makes the rest of this section obvious, so it is
worth two minutes.

| Thing | What it is | Where you see it |
|-------|------------|------------------|
| **Carb entry** | "35 g of carbs at 12:41." A dated amount, and nothing more. | The graph, **Meals** |
| **Food** | A reusable item in your catalog: _Greek yogurt, 12 g carbs per 100 g_. | **Food** |
| **Attribution** | A line saying _this_ carb entry was 1.5 portions of _that_ food. | **Meals**, expanded |
| **Meal** | A carb entry and the insulin given for it, grouped as one event. | **Meals** |

The split matters because the pieces arrive at different times and from
different places. Your pump uploads the carb entry the moment you bolus. The
food catalog is something you build up over months. The attribution — what the
carbs actually _were_ — may be added days later, by you or by a food-logging app
you have connected. None of them has to wait for the others.

## How they fit together

A carb entry stands on its own. It is complete and useful with no food attached
to it: the graph draws it, carbs-on-board counts it, reports total it up.

Attributions sit on top. Each one names a food from your catalog, a number of
portions, and the grams of carb that food contributed. Add them up and you get
the **attributed** carbs for that entry; whatever is left over is **unspecified**.
An entry with nothing attributed is 100% unspecified, and that is a perfectly
normal state for it to stay in forever.

A meal is the grouping layer. When you bolus for food, the insulin and the carbs
are recorded as two records that share a correlation identifier, and Nocturne
shows them as a single meal event. A carb entry with no insulin correlated to it
is still a meal event; it just has no insulin in it.

<Callout type="info" title="Why not one record?">
  Carbs and insulin come from different places, get corrected independently, and
  are used by different calculations. Keeping them separate means a corrected
  bolus does not rewrite your carb history, and a carb entry that arrives from
  your phone can still be joined to a bolus that arrived from your pump.
</Callout>

## The pages

- **[Carb entries](/docs/food/carbs)** — what a carb entry holds, the ways one
  gets created, and the fields loops and pumps add to it.
- **[The food catalog](/docs/food/catalog)** — building your list of foods,
  portions and quick picks, and what happens when you delete one that is in use.
- **[Meals and food attribution](/docs/food/meals)** — the Meals page,
  attributing carbs to foods, and matching entries from a food-logging app.
- **[When the same carbs arrive twice](/docs/food/deduplication)** — how
  Nocturne collapses the duplicate that appears when two apps report the same
  meal.

## The short version

If you only read one thing: log carbs however is convenient, from as many apps
as you like. Nocturne works out which of them are the same event and shows you
one. Attribute them to foods later if you want the detail, or never, if you
don't.
